  - vision-touch-audio
abstract: >
  VisTouch contains synchronized video, audio, and haptic (tactile force)
  recordings captured by controlling a robot arm with a dexterous hand to
  press and slide across everyday materials, achieving strict event-level
  time synchronization across all three modalities for the same physical
  contact event. The full corpus comprises more than 1000 multimodal signal
  pairs across 47 material categories; this public release covers the 8
  categories used in the evaluation of the companion paper (brass, linen,
  paper, polyester, silk, spandex, stone, wood), packaged as 312 strictly
  non-overlapping, phase-labeled audio/tactile/video source segments that
  tile 24 continuous recording sessions, further materialized into 10498
  two-second tri-modal clips derived by synchronously interpolating real
  0.5-second windows sampled at a 0.15-second stride, and indexed into
  51177 frame-level tri-modal samples, with a predefined cross-pressure
  train/test split. Source segments are genuine sensor captures; derived
  clips are deterministic interpolations, not simulated or model-generated
  recordings.
